# Git & GitHub — One-Page Developer Cheat Sheet

## 1. First-Time Git Setup

```bash
git config --global user.name "Your Name"
git config --global user.email "your@email.com"

git config --global --list
```

---

## 2. Create / Start a Repository

### Create a new local repository

```bash
git init
```

### Clone an existing GitHub repository

```bash
git clone <repository-url>
cd <repository-name>
```

---

## 3. Check Your Project

```bash
git status
```

Shows:

* Modified files
* New files
* Deleted files
* Staged files
* Current branch

### View commit history

```bash
git log
```

Short version:

```bash
git log --oneline
```

---

## 4. Add Changes

### Add one file

```bash
git add filename
```

### Add everything

```bash
git add .
```

### Unstage a file

```bash
git restore --staged filename
```

---

## 5. Commit Changes

```bash
git commit -m "Add payment model"
```

Good commit messages describe **what changed**.

Examples:

```bash
git commit -m "Add product API"
git commit -m "Fix cart calculation"
git commit -m "Add JWT authentication"
git commit -m "Update README"
```

---

# 6. Connect Local Project to GitHub

```bash
git remote add origin <repository-url>
```

Check remote:

```bash
git remote -v
```

---

# 7. Push Code to GitHub

First push:

```bash
git push -u origin main
```

Later:

```bash
git push
```

---

# 8. Get Latest Changes

```bash
git pull
```

Pull from a specific branch:

```bash
git pull origin main
```

---

# 9. Branches

### See branches

```bash
git branch
```

### Create branch

```bash
git branch feature-name
```

### Switch branch

```bash
git switch feature-name
```

### Create + switch

```bash
git switch -c feature-name
```

Example:

```bash
git switch -c payment-api
```

### Delete local branch

```bash
git branch -d feature-name
```

---

# 10. Merge

Switch to the branch you want to merge into:

```bash
git switch main
```

Then:

```bash
git merge feature-name
```

---

# 11. Undo Changes

### Discard changes in a file

```bash
git restore filename
```

### Unstage changes

```bash
git restore --staged filename
```

### Undo last commit but keep changes

```bash
git reset --soft HEAD~1
```

### Completely remove the last commit and changes

```bash
git reset --hard HEAD~1
```

⚠️ Be careful with `--hard`.

---

# 12. Stash

Temporarily save unfinished work:

```bash
git stash
```

See stashes:

```bash
git stash list
```

Restore latest stash:

```bash
git stash pop
```

---

# 13. Compare Changes

```bash
git diff
```

Compare staged changes:

```bash
git diff --staged
```

---

# 14. Remote Branches

```bash
git branch -a
```

Fetch latest remote information:

```bash
git fetch
```

Fetch and then merge:

```bash
git pull
```

---

# 15. Tags / Releases

Create a version tag:

```bash
git tag v1.0.0
```

Push tag:

```bash
git push origin v1.0.0
```

---

# 16. Remove Files

Delete file through Git:

```bash
git rm filename
```

Rename file:

```bash
git mv oldname newname
```

---

# 17. GitHub Developer Workflow

This is the workflow you'll use constantly:

```text
CREATE / CLONE PROJECT
        ↓
   git status
        ↓
    MAKE CHANGES
        ↓
     git diff
        ↓
     git add .
        ↓
git commit -m "message"
        ↓
      git push
        ↓
      GitHub
```

For an existing project:

```bash
git pull
# Make changes

git status
git diff
git add .
git commit -m "Describe change"
git push
```

---

# 18. Feature Development Workflow

For a larger feature:

```bash
git pull origin main

git switch -c payment-api

# Develop feature

git status
git add .
git commit -m "Add payment API"

git push -u origin payment-api
```

Then create a **Pull Request on GitHub**.

After review:

```text
Feature Branch
      ↓
Pull Request
      ↓
Code Review
      ↓
Merge
      ↓
main
```

---

